{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JeGQzgGeCDCPW5TbdteRJn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand what needs to be done.\n\n## Project Summary\n\nThis is a Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The project follows a specification-driven development approach where SRDs (Software Requirements Documents) are written first, then code is generated by an AI agent.\n\n## Current State\n\nLooking at the `requirement_states` in the project context, I can see which SRDs have requirements in \"ready\" state (meaning they need implementation). Let me also cross-reference with the `code_status` to understand what's already implemented.\n\n\nFrom the code status, I can see there are quite a few use cases that haven't been started yet—these include the format and testutils packages, along with several command implementations like cat, nproc, ls variants, logname, echo, tac, and expand.\n\nLooking at the additional items that need to be started, there's a substantial list spanning from cut through to the rel11 release, covering utilities like sha1sum, sha512sum, mktemp, groups, sort, mv, sleep, factor, various checksum commands, base32, stat, truncate, and link.\n\nThen moving into rel11 and beyond, I see mknod, nice, who, and a cluster of utilities in rel12 through rel15 including shred, chroot, install, tsort, pathchk, test, df, dir, vdir, dircolors, chronic, pee, vidir, ts, stty, pr, and ptx.\n\nChecking the requirement states, only a few SRDs have documented requirements so far—srd004-ts is fully ready, srd099-shred is fully ready, and srd100-chroot has R2.1, R2.2, and R2.3 ready, though the R1.x requirements aren't listed yet.\n\nThe rest of the SRDs from srd101 through srd114 all show everything ready, but I'm noticing a gap—the constraint requires proposing tasks only for R-items marked ready, yet the requirement_states only covers specific SRDs while there are many more utilities in the release list that don't appear to have documented requirements at all. Looking at the git history, the most recent work was on srd100-chroot and srd097-who, and I can see from requirement_states that srd100-chroot has R1 items already handled but R2 items still marked as ready. The requirement_states essentially tracks what's left to do—SRDs not listed there have completed all their requirements. So I should focus only on the SRDs that appear in requirement_states with ready status. Now I'm mapping each SRD to its release to apply release-driven priority and identify which release has the most incomplete work. Looking at the earliest incomplete release with ready requirements, that's rel12.0 with shred, chroot, and install—totaling 25 requirements across those three tasks. Now I need to verify the weight budgets to see if these can actually fit in the release.\n\nSince I don't have access to the actual weights from requirements.yaml and can't use tools, I'll work with the default assumption of 1 weight per requirement. That means srd099-shred and srd101-install would each hit the 4-weight limit with their 11 requirements, so I'd need to select subsets that stay within budget.\n\nFor srd100-chroot, the 3 remaining requirements fit cleanly into one task at weight 3. For srd099-shred, I can group the first four requirements (R1.1 through R1.4) into a single task at weight 4, then handle the remaining seven requirements in subsequent tasks. is tracking the actual implementation status, so I need to verify what's actually been done versus what the system thinks has been done. The 4-task limit means I can't fit all the work, so I need to prioritize completing chroot first, then move into shred's initial tasks while keeping an eye on whether the contracts already exist in the codebase. I'm working through the task ordering more carefully now. The contract task needs dependency -1, and implementation tasks should depend on it. So I'd structure it as: chroot R2.1-R2.3 at index 0, shred contract at index 1, then shred implementation tasks at indices 2 and 3, with each implementation task depending on the contract task at index 1.\n\nBut I need to reconsider the canonical ordering from article P8—contracts come before implementations, and within the same release, tasks are ordered numerically by file path. Since srd099-shred comes before srd100-chroot, the shred contract should actually come first, followed by the chroot task, then the shred implementations. The ordering looks correct now—contracts first, then implementations alphabetically by file path. But I realize I don't have access to the actual SRD requirement documents themselves, only the requirement states and project context, so I'll need to figure out what each requirement entails before writing the task descriptions. use any tools. Do NOT explore the filesystem, read files, or run commands. All project information is in the project_context above.\"\n\nSo I need to work with what I have. Let me compose reasonable task descriptions based on the roadmap descriptions and what I know about these utilities.\n\nFrom the roadmap:\n- rel12.0-uc001-shred: \"shred overwrites files with random data to prevent recovery\"\n- rel12.0-uc002-chroot: \"chroot changes the root directory and executes commands in the new root\"\n- rel12.0-uc003-install: \"install copies files while setting ownership, permissions, and creating directories\"\n\nFor the SRDs, the required_reading should point to the actual SRD files. The naming convention is `docs/specs/software-requirements/srdNNN-name.yaml`.\n\nFor chroot (srd100-chroot), the remaining requirements are R2.1, R2.2, R2.3. Looking at the previous commit about the core implementation, R1 covered the basic functionality while R2 likely handles additional features, error handling, or testing. For shred (srd099-shred), all requirements are ready—R1 probably covers core overwrite functionality, R2 additional features like iterations and removal, and R3 error handling or edge cases. I need to make sure the task descriptions are self-contained since the stitch agent only sees them, and I should include required_reading pointing to the SRD files. the context, they specify exact signatures. But for srd099-shred, there is no pre-defined contract in package_contracts, so I need to derive the contract directly from the SRD itself. The constraint is clear: before implementing a new package, I should propose a contract task that defines all exported types, interfaces, and function signatures as Go stubs. For cmd/shred specifically, the contract is essentially the command-line interface itself since cmd packages don't typically export types the way pkg packages do.\n\nFor cmd/ packages, the contract task would establish the initial main.go with flag parsing setup and the basic structure for the main logic functions. This skeleton serves as the foundation that implementation tasks then build upon, even though there aren't traditional exported interfaces to define like there would be for a library package.\n\nSince the code_status shows \"not started\" for shred and all requirements are ready, I'll treat it as not yet existing. I'm laying out the task sequence: first the shred contract task to create the cmd/shred/main.go stub, then the chroot continuation tasks for the remaining requirements.\n\nNow I need to verify the weight budgets for each step. Without the actual weights from requirements.yaml, I'm assuming weight 1 for each requirement as the default. Task 1 with R2.1, R2.2, and R2.3 totals 3 weight, which fits within the 4-weight limit.  Task 2 with R1.1 through R1.4 totals 4 weight, exactly at the limit. Task 3 with R2.1 through R2.4 also totals 4 weight, right at the boundary. All tasks are within budget.\n\nI should really check the SRD to get the actual requirement weights, but I'll