s an existing package implementation task, then shred R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.4 as a new package implementation task that creates main.go, with the second shred task depending on the first.\n\nActually, the constraint says \"Do not propose implementation tasks for a package that has no contract task unless the package already exists in source_code.\" Shred doesn't exist yet, so I need a contract task first.\n\nWith 4 tasks and needing a contract for shred, I'll structure it as: shred contract task, then chroot implementation, then shred implementation split across two tasks with the second depending on the first.\n\nFor the contract task, the stitch agent needs to create the main entry point with package declaration, imports, flag variables, and stub functions for the overwrite logic. Since I don't have the SRD content, I'll instruct the agent to read it and generate stubs based on what it finds. I should also make sure the task titles include the requirement IDs as specified in the constraints.\n\nNow I'm laying out the four tasks with their dependencies and requirement groupings. The contract task comes first with no dependencies, followed by the chroot implementation, then two separate batches of shred implementation work covering different requirement sets. I need to write clear YAML descriptions that tell the stitch agent what to build for each phase.\n\nFor the contract, I should guide the agent to read the SRD, extract all the command flags and behaviors, scaffold out main.go with stub functions, and handle the SIGPIPE signal along with flag parsing. For chroot, the agent needs to build on the existing implementation by adding the more advanced features like error handling and user specification options that weren't in the first phase. The shred tasks follow a similar pattern—the first batch covers the core overwrite logic and file selection, while the second batch adds the optional features and refinements.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the specifics of what R2 for shred should include: iterations control, zero-pass mode, file removal, and verbose output. I also need to verify the file paths match the SRD naming convention and confirm that the cmd directories are structured correctly. One more consideration—the tests should be separate from the implementation tasks, so the implementation work shouldn't include test file creation, but the acceptance criteria should define what the tests need to verify. I need to reconsider the contract task requirement. The constraint says to propose a contract task before implementation for any new package, not just pkg/ packages. Even though cmd/ packages don't export APIs, the contract would define the flag interface and internal function signatures. I'll include the contract task in the output.\n\nI'm also reconsidering the weight budget—I don't have the requirements.yaml content, so I'll assume weight 1 for all tasks and note that assumption. The earlier roadmap references suggest some requirements have higher weights, but for rel12.0 the tasks likely all have weight 1.\n\nNow I'm ready to finalize the output with four tasks and ensure my descriptions are clear. against GNU reference binary (g-prefixed Homebrew). Looking at the current state, I have 33 releases completed with spec_complete status, though generation run 44 is still in progress. The recent work on srd100-chroot and srd097-who shows I'm tracking remaining ready requirements across multiple SRDs. Release 12.0 stands out as the earliest with pending work—chroot needs R2.1 through R2.3 finished, while both shred and install have all 11 requirements ready to implement.\n\nFor the priority sequence with my 4-task limit, I should complete the three remaining chroot requirements first since that package already exists from the previous task, then move into shred which needs a contract established before implementation. The weight budget shows chroot's three requirements total 3 weight units, leaving capacity for starting shred's contract work.\n\nNow I'm working through the YAML structure for these tasks, making sure each description is clear and self-contained for the stitch agent to execute properly. I'm realizing that skipping the contract task would prevent me from proposing implementation tasks for the shred package, so I need to include it. For a cmd/ package, the contract should define the main.go skeleton with all internal function signatures as stubs and flag variable declarations—even though these aren't technically exported, they establish the structure the implementation needs to follow.\n\nNow I'm working through the title format based on the existing pattern. The constraint specifies adding a \"(contract)\" suffix, so I need to structure it as \"[stitch] cmd/shred (contract) -- srd099-shred\" to match the established naming convention.\n\nI'm also thinking about what R2.1-R2.3 might cover for the chroot implementation task, since the current main.go already handles R1.1-R1.3. Let me finalize the output with these task titles and then consider what additional requirements the second phase might involve. I'm working through a constraint conflict here—the planning constitution explicitly states that a single task can't both implement and test the same feature, but in this project the differential tests seem integral to the implementation deliverable. I need to follow the rule strictly: implementation tasks will only create main.go, and test tasks will be separate and come in the next batch. With four tasks available, I'll allocate them as one contract, two implementation tasks, and one chroot continuation task, leaving room for dedicated test tasks later.\n\nFor acceptance criteria on the implementation tasks without tests, I should focus on code quality and completeness rather than just \"compiles\"—things like successful compilation, adherence to project patterns like the SIGPIPE handler and flag parsing, and verification that all SRD requirements are actually addressed in the implementation.\n\nThe test tasks would then be separate, covering differential tests against the reference binary and ensuring the comparison succeeds.\n\nI'm also thinking through whether the chroot continuation task should include test modifications—since P2 says implementation and tests must be separate, I should only add the R2 implementation to main.go and leave test additions for a dedicated test task.\n\nFor the required_reading sections, I need to point the stitch agent toward the SRD file for that specific tool, the existing implementation code to understand what's already there, the architecture patterns, and maybe an existing cmd package as a reference. The CLAUDE.md and project rules are already loaded, so I don't need to repeat those. I'm identifying the key design decisions for each task: the contract task needs to follow the project's SIGPIPE handler pattern and use Go's standard flag package with TODO stubs for each requirement group, chroot continuation should build on the existing implementation while matching GNU chroot's --userspec and --groups behavior, and shred R1 requires the cmd/ pattern with crypto/rand for generating random data.\n\nFor shred R2, I'll build on the R1 core implementation. But I need to be careful here—the constraint is clear that I shouldn't invent design decisions without SRD backing. Since I don't have the actual SRD content, I should only derive decisions from what the architecture document specifies: all cmd/ utilities must call the SIGPIPE handler, and there's a differential testing pattern to follow. Anything beyond that would be speculation.\n\nThe shared protocols mention LC_ALL=C for tests and reference binary lookup with t.Skip, but those are testing concerns, not design decisions for the implementation itself. So my actual design decisions should stay minimal: call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at main startup per the project protocol, and use the standard flag package for CLI arguments. The stitch agent will fill